WEBVTT

00:00:07.507 --> 00:00:09.509
["Moonlight" by L'FREAQ playing]

00:00:10.969 --> 00:00:15.765
♪ Paint my wounds, lover... ♪

00:00:15.849 --> 00:00:18.810
[sister] My sister Deva loves
really deeply,

00:00:18.893 --> 00:00:21.604
but ever since a couple of heartbreaks,

00:00:21.688 --> 00:00:25.275
I haven't really seen her
surrender to romance.

00:00:26.401 --> 00:00:30.113
I think she's trying
to relearn her heart, in a way.

00:00:33.491 --> 00:00:37.662
♪ Ho, oh, don't you want to see me
In the moonlight? ♪

00:00:38.163 --> 00:00:41.624
[sister] When people meet Deva,
they are pretty intimidated by her.

00:00:41.708 --> 00:00:44.335
She needs someone
who is confident enough in themselves

00:00:44.419 --> 00:00:45.920
to embrace her in all her glory.

00:00:47.172 --> 00:00:53.553
♪ Ho, oh,
Say you'll never treat me nice... ♪

00:00:54.721 --> 00:00:56.556
[sister] She definitely has her guards.

00:00:56.639 --> 00:00:58.641
But since Deva's moved to New Orleans,

00:00:58.725 --> 00:01:02.103
I have seen her own her sexuality,

00:01:02.187 --> 00:01:04.564
and her beauty, and her body.

00:01:06.649 --> 00:01:11.738
♪ Ho, oh, don't you recognize me
In the moonlight? ♪

00:01:14.282 --> 00:01:16.076
[sister] I'd love to see her have

00:01:16.159 --> 00:01:18.536
a joyful, romantic experience
with someone...

00:01:21.122 --> 00:01:24.709
where they can completely fall in love
with abandon...

00:01:25.627 --> 00:01:28.546
rather than compete for space.

00:01:34.886 --> 00:01:37.347
-[chuckles] Hi.
-Hey, how you doing?

00:01:37.430 --> 00:01:38.932
-I'm Rueben.
-Good. I'm Deva.

00:01:39.015 --> 00:01:40.600
-Nice to meet you. Deva?
-Deva, yeah.

00:01:40.683 --> 00:01:42.727
-Okay. You look amazing.
-Thank you.

00:01:42.811 --> 00:01:44.771
-Tell me your name one more time. Rueben.
-Rueben.

00:01:45.271 --> 00:01:47.232
-It's a pleasure to meet you.
-Cool.

00:01:47.649 --> 00:01:49.734
Uh, I think, should we go get a drink?

00:01:49.818 --> 00:01:51.194
-Yeah, let's go in.
-Okay.

00:02:03.456 --> 00:02:05.792
So, True.

00:02:05.875 --> 00:02:06.709
Yes.

00:02:06.793 --> 00:02:09.045
It's very nice to meet you.
You look really nice tonight.

00:02:09.128 --> 00:02:11.422
Thank you.
I put it all together for this date.

00:02:11.506 --> 00:02:12.674
Oh, well.

00:02:12.757 --> 00:02:13.925
You look amazing too.

00:02:14.008 --> 00:02:15.718
-Thank you.
-No doubt. No doubt.

00:02:15.802 --> 00:02:17.387
What's...? Who's...?

00:02:17.470 --> 00:02:20.348
I mean, I didn't mean to touch you
or anything like that.

00:02:20.431 --> 00:02:21.933
-Ask consent always.
-[laughs]

00:02:22.016 --> 00:02:23.434
But it's Ulla Johnson.

00:02:23.518 --> 00:02:25.186
She's an amazing designer from New York.

00:02:25.270 --> 00:02:26.688
Oh. I'm from New York.

00:02:26.771 --> 00:02:27.856
Really? Where?

00:02:27.939 --> 00:02:28.982
I'm from Marshall Heights.

00:02:29.065 --> 00:02:30.275
I used to live in Harlem.

00:02:30.358 --> 00:02:31.860
-No doubt.
-Yeah.

00:02:31.943 --> 00:02:34.612
You're definitely from New York
'cause you're like, "No doubt."

00:02:34.696 --> 00:02:36.197
-Like, I see you.
-[laughs]

00:02:45.331 --> 00:02:47.333
-Thank you.
-[Maria] Hi, are you my date?

00:02:47.417 --> 00:02:48.251
Yeah.

00:02:48.334 --> 00:02:50.044
-Hi. I'm Maria. Good to meet you.
-Hi.

00:02:50.128 --> 00:02:51.379
-Can I hug you?
-I'm Deva. Yeah.

00:02:51.462 --> 00:02:52.922
-Devee? Deva.
-Deva.

00:02:53.506 --> 00:02:54.591
Maria.

00:02:54.674 --> 00:02:55.758
Good to meet you.

00:02:56.885 --> 00:02:57.802
You look great.

00:02:57.886 --> 00:02:59.429
-You look great.
-Thank you.

00:03:00.346 --> 00:03:01.180
Cool.

00:03:01.264 --> 00:03:02.724
How are you?

00:03:02.807 --> 00:03:04.100
-Doing fine.
-Yeah.

00:03:04.184 --> 00:03:05.894
-Had a nice day off.
-Oh, you did?

00:03:05.977 --> 00:03:08.188
Yeah, some relaxing porch lunch

00:03:08.271 --> 00:03:10.773
-and chilled in the garden.
-Cool.

00:03:11.441 --> 00:03:13.234
Nice. Oh!

00:03:13.318 --> 00:03:14.903
It's so nice to see you.

00:03:15.695 --> 00:03:18.656
I went and got acupuncture
for the first time before I came here.

00:03:19.157 --> 00:03:20.575
I'd never done it before.

00:03:20.658 --> 00:03:22.660
And I was like, it just,
like, ended up being,

00:03:22.744 --> 00:03:24.996
like, right before I had to be here.

00:03:25.079 --> 00:03:25.955
Yeah.

00:03:26.039 --> 00:03:29.167
-And it was so, like, calming.
-Oh, good. I should have done that.

00:03:29.792 --> 00:03:30.919
Right.

00:03:33.379 --> 00:03:35.089
I suddenly was so chill,

00:03:35.173 --> 00:03:38.718
and I suddenly just got super nervous
when you walked in. [chuckles]

00:03:38.801 --> 00:03:40.720
-I already know what I want.
-So do I.

00:03:40.803 --> 00:03:41.846
I want a green tea shot.

00:03:41.930 --> 00:03:43.222
What is a green tea shot?

00:03:43.306 --> 00:03:45.600
Well, I'm trying to drink healthier.

00:03:45.683 --> 00:03:47.143
Oh, like actual green tea.

00:03:47.227 --> 00:03:48.394
No, it's not actual green tea.

00:03:48.478 --> 00:03:50.313
There's, like, Jameson Whiskey, uh...

00:03:50.396 --> 00:03:52.065
I think peach schnapps,

00:03:52.190 --> 00:03:54.817
um, sour and Sprite.

00:03:54.901 --> 00:03:56.486
How is that healthier?

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:58.529
It has a lot of antioxidants.

00:03:59.072 --> 00:04:00.323
Which is great for--

00:04:00.406 --> 00:04:01.908
In the Sprite or the Jameson?

00:04:01.991 --> 00:04:05.411
-What are you drinking?
-I'll have a white Negroni with mezcal.

00:04:05.495 --> 00:04:07.622
I'll have a white maroni with mezcal.

00:04:08.206 --> 00:04:09.582
Negroni. [laughs]

00:04:10.375 --> 00:04:12.001
Did you say maroni?

00:04:12.126 --> 00:04:14.003
-That's what you said.
-No, I did not.

00:04:14.087 --> 00:04:15.922
-I'll have what she's having.
-Okay.

00:04:16.005 --> 00:04:17.298
[laughing]

00:04:18.508 --> 00:04:20.051
Tell me a little bit about yourself.

00:04:21.302 --> 00:04:22.470
I grew up in Los Angeles.

00:04:23.012 --> 00:04:27.767
And I got to the point where I recognized
that the city was going to kill me.

00:04:28.268 --> 00:04:29.352
Oh, okay.

00:04:29.435 --> 00:04:32.355
-At least spiritually, if not physically.
-Okay.

00:04:32.438 --> 00:04:35.108
And I went to New York and...

00:04:35.191 --> 00:04:37.568
made something of myself,

00:04:37.652 --> 00:04:40.196
and then got tired of New York
and moved here.

00:04:40.280 --> 00:04:41.197
Okay.

00:04:41.948 --> 00:04:44.033
Are you, um, originally from here?

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:45.326
No.

00:04:45.410 --> 00:04:47.412
Originally I am from Hawaii.

00:04:47.495 --> 00:04:48.329
Oh. Cool.

00:04:48.413 --> 00:04:51.291
So I left and I was in
the Pacific Northwest for a while.

00:04:51.374 --> 00:04:54.210
And then I moved to New Zealand...

00:04:54.294 --> 00:04:55.211
Mm-hmm.

00:04:55.294 --> 00:04:57.797
...for, like, almost seven years.

00:04:57.880 --> 00:04:59.799
-So, why did you go to New Zealand?
-Well--

00:04:59.882 --> 00:05:02.301
It's like the growth on the neck
of Australia?

00:05:02.385 --> 00:05:04.304
-Don't say that. You can't say that.
-I'm sorry.

00:05:04.387 --> 00:05:06.973
I have too much New Zealand pride
for that to slide.

00:05:08.516 --> 00:05:09.684
When did you move here?

00:05:09.767 --> 00:05:11.227
Four years for me in January.

00:05:11.311 --> 00:05:13.646
-So whenever that is, 2000--
-Oh. I'm way fresher than you.

00:05:13.730 --> 00:05:15.440
-I moved in December.
-You're fresh to death?

00:05:15.523 --> 00:05:17.191
-You're super fresh.
-Straight.

00:05:17.275 --> 00:05:18.568
-Straight fresh.
-Straight fresh.

00:05:18.651 --> 00:05:19.861
-Off the boat.
-No doubt.

00:05:20.653 --> 00:05:21.821
[Maria] Why did you come here?

00:05:22.697 --> 00:05:23.698
I, uh...

00:05:23.781 --> 00:05:25.533
wanted a change of pace.

00:05:25.616 --> 00:05:29.245
New York is really challenging to live in,
and also be creative.

00:05:29.329 --> 00:05:31.414
-I just put out a record last year.
-Oh, great.

00:05:31.497 --> 00:05:34.917
So that's been exciting.
I've spent most of the year touring.

00:05:35.001 --> 00:05:37.795
So now you're a singer extraordinaire.

00:05:37.879 --> 00:05:40.298
-I mean--
-How did you get into doing that?

00:05:40.882 --> 00:05:44.344
When I was a kid I was, like, pretty--
I didn't really fit in.

00:05:44.927 --> 00:05:47.305
And it was hard for me to make friends.

00:05:47.388 --> 00:05:48.431
There was, like,

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.017
some bullying and all those kinds
of things when I was growing up.

00:05:51.100 --> 00:05:55.104
And then I just discovered
I really loved making music.

00:05:55.646 --> 00:05:56.981
Like, so deeply.

00:05:57.482 --> 00:06:01.361
Well, maybe a little later on
I'm gonna have to hear a little something.

00:06:01.444 --> 00:06:03.112
-Oh, no.
-If you're open to it.

00:06:03.196 --> 00:06:04.864
-No.
-No? I've gotta pay for it?

00:06:04.947 --> 00:06:07.075
Yeah, you've gotta pay for it.
That costs money.

00:06:07.992 --> 00:06:10.787
-People gotta pay for my stuff, only fair.
-True. What do you do?

00:06:10.870 --> 00:06:11.704
I'm a chiropractor.

00:06:11.788 --> 00:06:13.706
-Oh, my God. We gotta talk.
-[chuckles]

00:06:13.790 --> 00:06:16.334
Often times I'm on the other side
of the bar, where he is.

00:06:16.417 --> 00:06:17.668
-Oh, you bartend?
-Yeah.

00:06:17.752 --> 00:06:19.879
I managed to get some properties.

00:06:19.962 --> 00:06:22.423
So I live off off of rental income.

00:06:23.132 --> 00:06:24.884
Wow. That's amazing.

00:06:26.010 --> 00:06:28.930
So I work for a ballet company.
I make costumes for them.

00:06:29.013 --> 00:06:30.473
My grandmother was a seamstress,

00:06:30.556 --> 00:06:32.892
and she would make all of my mom
and her sisters' clothes.

00:06:32.975 --> 00:06:33.810
Whoa.

00:06:33.893 --> 00:06:35.645
So after she died I feel like I, like,

00:06:35.728 --> 00:06:37.855
inherited her sewing juju somehow.

00:06:37.939 --> 00:06:38.981
Wow.

00:06:39.065 --> 00:06:42.193
I mean, New Orleans
is such a costume town, you know?

00:06:42.276 --> 00:06:44.487
Like, anytime you go out,
like, every weekend,

00:06:44.570 --> 00:06:46.531
-it's like, you know, looks.
-I love it.

00:06:46.614 --> 00:06:48.074
-I love it. I love it.
-[laughs]

00:06:48.157 --> 00:06:49.909
-Well, cheers. Yeah.
-Cheers, darling.

00:06:51.869 --> 00:06:52.745
All right.

00:06:52.829 --> 00:06:54.038
Salut. Salut.

00:06:55.373 --> 00:06:56.332
-Cheers.
-Cheers.

00:06:56.958 --> 00:06:58.167
Oh! Careful.

00:06:59.210 --> 00:07:01.129
[waiter] Your table's ready
whenever you're ready.

00:07:01.212 --> 00:07:02.255
-[Deva] Thank you.
-Follow me.

00:07:02.338 --> 00:07:04.173
-Are you ready to eat?
-Yeah. [chuckles]

00:07:04.757 --> 00:07:06.717
-How's your drink?
-It's good, yeah.

00:07:06.801 --> 00:07:08.511
I love me an old fashioned.

00:07:08.594 --> 00:07:10.471
-Thank you. Thank you.
-Awesome.

00:07:18.229 --> 00:07:20.189
Thanks. [chuckles]

00:07:25.778 --> 00:07:26.654
So...

00:07:28.239 --> 00:07:29.574
Tell me about your artwork.

00:07:29.657 --> 00:07:30.700
[Maria] Oh.

00:07:30.783 --> 00:07:33.661
-I just had an art show, actually.
-Oh, you did?

00:07:33.744 --> 00:07:36.747
Last week. I have a gallery show
up on Julia Street.

00:07:36.831 --> 00:07:38.708
-What's your last name?
-Sandhammer.

00:07:39.250 --> 00:07:40.793
I know your stuff.

00:07:40.877 --> 00:07:42.587
-No, you don't.
-Yeah.

00:07:42.670 --> 00:07:44.338
-Do you make eye masks?
-I do that too.

00:07:44.422 --> 00:07:46.090
-Yeah.
-I do that too.

00:07:46.174 --> 00:07:47.341
-Those are awesome.
-[laughs]

00:07:47.425 --> 00:07:48.885
-Thank you.
-Yeah.

00:07:48.968 --> 00:07:50.428
I bought one for my roommate.

00:07:50.511 --> 00:07:52.180
-Cool. You bought one? [laughs]
-Yeah.

00:07:52.263 --> 00:07:53.306
Yeah, totally.

00:07:54.265 --> 00:07:55.266
That's cool.

00:07:55.933 --> 00:07:58.895
Good evening, guys, my name is Scott.
I'll take care of you tonight. Welcome.

00:07:58.978 --> 00:08:00.730
-Here you are. Very welcome.
-Thank you.

00:08:00.813 --> 00:08:03.941
I would like to try the olive-cured duck.

00:08:04.025 --> 00:08:05.276
[True] Perfect. Good choice.

00:08:05.359 --> 00:08:07.445
-Could I get the redfish, please?
-[Scott] Redfish, excellent.

00:08:07.862 --> 00:08:08.738
No worries.

00:08:10.448 --> 00:08:11.282
When's your birthday?

00:08:11.365 --> 00:08:13.326
-I'm an Aries, unfortunately.
-Okay.

00:08:13.409 --> 00:08:14.702
Why unfortunately?

00:08:14.785 --> 00:08:16.287
Not a lot of people like us.

00:08:16.370 --> 00:08:17.830
I'm cool with Aries.

00:08:17.914 --> 00:08:20.166
-So that's cool. That's good to know.
-Yeah.

00:08:20.249 --> 00:08:22.168
-How about yourself? Capricorn.
-I'm a Capricorn.

00:08:22.251 --> 00:08:24.337
You're on the heels of Scorpios,

00:08:24.420 --> 00:08:26.589
who are like the bane of my existence.
[chuckles]

00:08:26.672 --> 00:08:30.134
No. It goes Scorpio,
Sagittarius, then Capricorn.

00:08:30.218 --> 00:08:31.135
Oh, okay, okay, okay.

00:08:32.720 --> 00:08:33.763
Oh, my God.

00:08:34.263 --> 00:08:35.556
That looks beautiful.

00:08:35.640 --> 00:08:37.308
It does look beautiful, so enjoy.

00:08:37.892 --> 00:08:38.935
[Deva] So, you share food?

00:08:39.936 --> 00:08:41.896
What's the point
of ordering different things--?

00:08:41.979 --> 00:08:44.815
-Some people don't like to share food.
-Some people kick puppies.

00:08:44.899 --> 00:08:46.901
-And... I'm just saying--
-Okay, where did you...

00:08:46.984 --> 00:08:49.111
-I don't.
-...where did we go from food sharing

00:08:49.195 --> 00:08:50.196
to kicking puppies?

00:08:50.279 --> 00:08:53.533
I'm not saying that people
that don't share food kick puppies.

00:08:53.616 --> 00:08:55.493
I'm just saying they're more likely to.

00:08:56.244 --> 00:08:58.538
[Maria] I really like my family,
for the most part.

00:08:58.621 --> 00:09:00.373
They all accept me,

00:09:00.456 --> 00:09:03.084
-even though I'm a weirdo.
-Oh, tell me about that.

00:09:03.167 --> 00:09:04.544
Are you, like, the black sheep?

00:09:04.627 --> 00:09:06.963
Yeah, yeah, pretty much. I--

00:09:07.046 --> 00:09:09.173
Is it because you date women
or is it because...

00:09:09.257 --> 00:09:10.967
-It's just because...
-...you're an artist?

00:09:11.050 --> 00:09:13.553
...I've always been-- My uncle's like,
"You're into art stuff."

00:09:13.636 --> 00:09:15.304
'Cause he's from Boston.

00:09:15.388 --> 00:09:16.639
"You're into that art stuff."

00:09:16.722 --> 00:09:19.850
I have family in that Boston area too.

00:09:19.934 --> 00:09:21.978
And it's very much like work,

00:09:22.061 --> 00:09:23.688
gotta have the house, the car,

00:09:23.771 --> 00:09:24.814
the kids.

00:09:24.897 --> 00:09:26.566
-Yes.
-So...

00:09:26.649 --> 00:09:29.110
So, yeah, I've had a little bit
of pressure surrounding that.

00:09:29.193 --> 00:09:31.654
-Yeah.
-[Brooke] There was one other person

00:09:31.737 --> 00:09:34.323
who I dated briefly in New Orleans.

00:09:34.407 --> 00:09:35.533
[Deva] Mm-hmm.

00:09:35.616 --> 00:09:38.077
Although they identified as a trans man.

00:09:38.160 --> 00:09:40.037
-Okay.
-So that was different.

00:09:40.121 --> 00:09:43.499
You know, there's, like, so much nuance
in, like, how people identify, like...

00:09:43.583 --> 00:09:46.544
What I'm just trying to do
is meet people where they're at

00:09:46.627 --> 00:09:50.506
and, like, not make any judgments
and really just support them, you know?

00:09:51.132 --> 00:09:52.842
[Deva] How long were you married for?

00:09:52.925 --> 00:09:54.677
I was with her for eight and a half years.

00:09:54.760 --> 00:09:56.846
Can I ask why you guys got divorced?

00:09:57.763 --> 00:09:59.932
-She wanted to be kept.
-Oh, okay.

00:10:00.016 --> 00:10:02.602
She wasn't saying to me,
"I don't want to work

00:10:02.685 --> 00:10:05.771
so that I can stay home and cook
and clean and bear your children."

00:10:05.855 --> 00:10:09.525
It was, "I don't wanna work
because I wanna live a fabulous life,

00:10:09.608 --> 00:10:12.236
and wear lots of new clothes,
and go to restaurants,

00:10:12.320 --> 00:10:13.738
and you pay for it.

00:10:13.821 --> 00:10:16.741
How do you feel dating a strong woman?

00:10:17.325 --> 00:10:18.993
-I'm attracted to that.
-Yeah?

00:10:19.076 --> 00:10:20.036
Yeah.

00:10:20.119 --> 00:10:22.496
I think a lot of people
are really intimidated by me.

00:10:23.080 --> 00:10:24.081
Don't find it...

00:10:25.207 --> 00:10:27.710
intimidating,
and a lot of times behind that...

00:10:28.377 --> 00:10:31.005
strongness is a sensitivity and...

00:10:32.214 --> 00:10:33.132
vulnerability.

00:10:34.133 --> 00:10:36.344
[both laughing]

00:10:36.594 --> 00:10:39.513
I think...
everybody on the planet is crazy.

00:10:39.597 --> 00:10:44.268
And crazy breaks down into two categories.

00:10:44.352 --> 00:10:47.104
You have homicidal crazy
and suicidal crazy.

00:10:48.522 --> 00:10:52.068
You know, the best you can hope for
is that you don't have a partner

00:10:52.151 --> 00:10:53.903
that will harm you.

00:10:55.196 --> 00:10:57.365
Okay, I'm trying
to follow you here, but...

00:10:57.448 --> 00:10:59.700
I think I need a little more...
[chuckles] explanation.

00:11:00.618 --> 00:11:02.620
Yeah, I've had a lot of, um...

00:11:03.537 --> 00:11:05.289
trying relationships.

00:11:06.832 --> 00:11:10.461
What about you?
What is your relationship background?

00:11:11.003 --> 00:11:14.590
I was with somebody for,
like, six and a half years.

00:11:15.299 --> 00:11:19.553
And that's probably the most
influential relationship I've ever had.

00:11:20.346 --> 00:11:23.432
And it was really challenging
to, like, move past that.

00:11:25.017 --> 00:11:28.104
I actually also have, kind of,
fully come to terms

00:11:28.187 --> 00:11:30.356
with the fact that I'm interested
